.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
#include \(dqbashclient.h\(dq

int bc_eval(bc_client_t *c, const char *command,
            bc_eval_result_t *result);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sends a shell command string to the \f[CR]bash\-server(1)\f[R] instance
for evaluation and collects the output.
The command is executed in the server\(cqs persistent shell environment,
so variables, functions, and working directory are preserved across
calls.
.PP
The function sends an \f[CR]eval\f[R] message on \f[CR]CHAN_COMMAND\f[R]
(channel 1) and then reads response messages until a \f[CR]complete\f[R]
message is received.
During execution, \f[CR]stdout\f[R] and \f[CR]stderr\f[R] messages are
accumulated into the result structure.
.PP
The \f[CR]result\f[R] parameter points to a caller\-provided
\f[CR]bc_eval_result_t\f[R] structure that is populated on success:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]typedef\f[R] \f[B]struct\f[R] {
    char *stdout_data;   \f[I]/* malloc\(aqd stdout output (NUL\-terminated) */\f[R]
    char *stderr_data;   \f[I]/* malloc\(aqd stderr output (NUL\-terminated) */\f[R]
    int   exit_code;     \f[I]/* command exit status ($?) */\f[R]
} bc_eval_result_t;
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]stdout_data\f[R] and \f[CR]stderr_data\f[R] fields are
heap\-allocated strings that must be freed by the caller using
\f[CR]bc_eval_result_free(3)\f[R] or \f[CR]bc_free(3)\f[R].
They are always NUL\-terminated and are set to an empty
\f[CR]malloc\f[R]\(cqd string (\f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]) if no output was
produced.
.PP
The \f[CR]exit_code\f[R] field contains the exit status of the command,
equivalent to \f[CR]$?\f[R] in the shell.
A value of 0 indicates success.
