.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
The command string is sent as\-is to the server\(cqs Bash interpreter.
Shell metacharacters, pipes, redirections, and compound commands all
work as expected.
.IP \(bu 2
The server executes commands by forking a child process.
stdout and stderr are captured via pipes and returned in the result
structure.
The command runs in the server\(cqs persistent shell environment.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]BC_OK\f[R] indicates that the protocol exchange completed, not
that the shell command succeeded.
Always check \f[CR]result\->exit_code\f[R] for the command\(cqs actual
exit status.
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[CR]stdout_data\f[R] and \f[CR]stderr_data\f[R] fields are always
non\-NULL after a successful call (they point to \f[CR]malloc\f[R]\(cqd
empty strings if no output was produced).
This makes it safe to use them with \f[CR]printf\f[R] without NULL
checks.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no hard limit on output size, but very large outputs (multiple
megabytes) may cause memory pressure.
For commands that produce large output, consider redirecting to a file
in the server\(cqs filesystem.
.IP \(bu 2
The function blocks until the command completes.
Long\-running commands will block the calling thread for the duration.
There is currently no asynchronous evaluation API.
.IP \(bu 2
Commands are executed sequentially.
Sending a second \f[CR]bc_eval\f[R] while the first is still running is
not supported and results in \f[CR]BC_ERR_PROTOCOL\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
The command\(cqs exit code is the value of \f[CR]$?\f[R] after
execution.
For pipelines, this is the exit code of the last command in the pipe
(unless \f[CR]pipefail\f[R] is set in the server\(cqs shell).
